WEBVTT

00:00:00.450 --> 00:00:06.465
We present AESOP: asymmetric human-camera generation with translation-intensity control.

00:00:08.450 --> 00:00:12.386
Given a human motion, AESOP generates its camera trajectory.

00:00:12.636 --> 00:00:16.595
For joint generation, it creates the human first, then the camera.

00:00:16.826 --> 00:00:22.985
Both tasks share one camera generator, with explicit control over how far the camera travels.

00:00:25.450 --> 00:00:27.433
Two observations motivate this design.

00:00:27.683 --> 00:00:32.386
Human motion defines the action; the camera determines how that action is presented.

00:00:32.636 --> 00:00:34.053
Their roles are asymmetric.

00:00:34.710 --> 00:00:42.688
Second, human motion provides the visual focus and camera text describes the movement, but the amount of camera travel remains underspecified.

00:00:42.938 --> 00:00:47.804
In the baseline example shown here, the weaker and stronger prompts produce similar displacement.

00:00:48.054 --> 00:00:53.247
We therefore model the dependency explicitly and learn intensity from geometric pairs.

00:00:54.350 --> 00:00:55.789
Here is the core idea.

00:00:57.350 --> 00:01:01.573
We first train a human encoder and decoder using human motion alone.

00:01:01.823 --> 00:01:05.001
The decoder reconstructs the motion from the human latent.

00:01:07.550 --> 00:01:11.432
We then freeze the human branch and train the camera representation.

00:01:11.682 --> 00:01:18.114
The camera decoder combines camera and human latents, using the action as context for reconstruction.

00:01:21.717 --> 00:01:29.961
This separation lets us encode a supplied motion without a camera trajectory, while still giving camera reconstruction access to the human action.

00:01:31.717 --> 00:01:36.664
Generative training follows the same human-first order as reconstruction training.

00:01:36.914 --> 00:01:44.349
We train a text-conditioned human flow to generate motion latents from noise, then freeze it before training the camera flow.

00:01:45.450 --> 00:01:49.929
We train the camera model on latents encoded from ground-truth human motion.

00:01:50.179 --> 00:01:56.468
At inference, supplied motions are encoded, while joint generation reuses the completed human latent.

00:01:56.718 --> 00:01:59.458
Both routes use the same camera flow and decoder.

00:02:00.617 --> 00:02:04.263
Direction-paired augmentation provides auxiliary supervision.

00:02:04.513 --> 00:02:09.493
We first select truck or dolly events with consistent direction and sufficient travel.

00:02:09.743 --> 00:02:15.651
We then construct opposite-direction trajectories and matching directional text for the same human motion.

00:02:15.901 --> 00:02:20.153
Finally, we retain pairs that pass geometry and reconstruction checks.

00:02:21.283 --> 00:02:28.589
Intensity-paired augmentation creates weaker and stronger camera trajectories with the same human motion and camera text.

00:02:28.839 --> 00:02:33.605
The construction scales smooth displacement without amplifying residual jitter.

00:02:33.855 --> 00:02:41.000
Geometry and reconstruction checks select the training pairs, which teach the model to respond to the intensity condition.

00:02:42.117 --> 00:02:43.800
We now examine the results.

00:02:45.117 --> 00:02:47.353
First, given-human camera generation.

00:02:47.603 --> 00:02:50.429
Each comparison uses the same supplied motion.

00:02:50.679 --> 00:02:58.027
In these examples, AESOP more closely follows the requested camera movement while maintaining clearer framing of the human action.

00:03:01.550 --> 00:03:04.835
The next example shows the same task with a different action.

00:03:19.550 --> 00:03:23.876
Next, joint human–camera generation starts from human and camera text.

00:03:24.126 --> 00:03:29.874
AESOP generates the action first, then conditions the camera trajectory on the completed motion.

00:03:35.050 --> 00:03:38.410
Here, both the action and camera trajectory are generated.

00:03:52.050 --> 00:03:57.817
Now we vary translation intensity while fixing the human motion, camera text, and camera noise.

00:03:58.067 --> 00:04:02.833
Camera travel increases across the five settings shown in these synchronized views.

00:04:07.750 --> 00:04:12.837
In joint generation, we use the same generated human motion at every intensity.

00:04:13.087 --> 00:04:17.791
Each setting regenerates the camera trajectory, changing its travel and framing.

00:04:22.350 --> 00:04:27.458
We next test the same model with motions and action descriptions from external sources.

00:04:27.708 --> 00:04:35.237
First, we adapt HumanML3D motions to AESOP’s representation and use them to condition camera generation.

00:04:36.350 --> 00:04:42.452
Here, we use an action description from HumanML3D instead of supplying the motion itself.

00:04:42.702 --> 00:04:46.648
AESOP generates both the human motion and the camera trajectory.

00:04:48.350 --> 00:04:52.996
Finally, we use human motions generated by HY-Motion as input.

00:04:53.246 --> 00:04:58.813
AESOP combines these motions with camera text to generate a camera trajectory for each motion.

00:05:01.950 --> 00:05:04.767
Reconstruction tests the representation itself.

00:05:05.017 --> 00:05:15.447
Compared with the symmetric PulpMotion representation, AESOP more closely recovers camera distribution and alignment, with lower human position and camera translation errors.

00:05:15.697 --> 00:05:19.088
These results support our human-first asymmetric design.

00:05:20.217 --> 00:05:26.851
For generation, AESOP achieves lower camera-distribution and framing-distribution distances in both tasks.

00:05:27.101 --> 00:05:38.151
Given-human projection-distribution distance is about fifty percent lower than DIRECTOR-C, and joint camera-distribution distance is about eighty-seven percent lower than PulpMotion DiT.

00:05:39.283 --> 00:05:45.885
These rows compare original-pair training, direction-pair continuation, and the final intensity-pair stage.

00:05:46.135 --> 00:05:54.059
Camera-distribution distance decreases across the stages, while the final model also supports the intensity control shown earlier.

00:05:55.183 --> 00:05:59.471
Learning from geometric pairs gives explicit translation-intensity control.

00:05:59.780 --> 00:06:04.706
AESOP unifies both tasks through an independent human pathway and a shared camera generator.

00:06:09.183 --> 00:06:10.109
Thank you.

00:06:10.359 --> 00:06:15.733
We hope AESOP inspires further work on controllable human-aware camera generation.
